                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1564-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_ms.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Mid-Side Stereo" title="Mid-Side Stereo" ></span>Nevaton MC52-MS</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Mid-Side Stereo Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The MC52 is a mid-side stereo microphone. It has extremely low-noise transformerless circuitry, and two large-diaphragm true condenser capsules.</p><p>One of the two capsules has a fixed Figure-8 pattern. The other has a fixed Omni pattern, although the mic can be ordered with Cardioid rather than Omni. The two capsules are mounted at a fixed 90&deg; to each other. The Cardioid (or Omni) faces front; the fig-8 faces side-to-side.</p><p>The capsules are a large-diaphragm center-terminated design, with an outside diameter of 33mm. They are shockmounted within the MC52 headbasket.</p><p>The MC52 includes a breakout cable, allowing the signals from the two capsules to be recorded to separate tracks. </p><p>The mic has no internal MS decoder; the user would have to set up a mixing matrix in the DAW or console in order to convert the two mono signals to a left/right stereo mix.</p><p>The mic was announced at Winter NAMM, January 2013.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Nevaton/MC52">Nevaton MC52-MS</a> is also known as: MC52-MS.</p><p>The mic was released in 2013.</p>
